<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 15px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # ___ – Lesson # 5</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 5</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Mediation</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 3rd Grade (CEFR: A1.1)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> This Beautiful Day</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> It's Sunny. I'm Happy!</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Mediation: Text: Interpret short, simple messages from class texts. Concept: Use simple analogies to explain basic concepts.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Mediation: Can interpret simple weather messages using gestures (e.g., points to the sun for "It's sunny."). Can explain weather concepts with simple analogies (e.g., "Rain is like a shower." and mimes rain). Can show understanding of weather words by pointing to visuals (e.g., points to an umbrella for "It's rainy.").</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead style="background-color: #f2f2f2;">
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 33%;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 33%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 34%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Engagement, Modeling and Clarification)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher starts by displaying large weather flashcards (sunny, rainy, cloudy, windy) and asks, "What's the weather like today?" and "How do you feel?" Students respond with simple sentences like "It's sunny. I'm happy." The teacher then points to a flashcard (e.g., "rainy") and asks, "How can you tell your friend 'It's rainy' if they don't understand the word?" encouraging gestures or mimes (e.g., mime falling rain, pointing to an umbrella). This pre-task directly engages the mediation skill by thinking about how to convey a message without just words.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models expressing weather and feelings using both words and gestures. "Look, it's sunny!" (points to the sun flashcard, makes a happy face). "I'm happy!" (gestures happiness). Then, "It's rainy!" (mimes rain, makes a slightly sad face). "I'm a little sad." The teacher demonstrates a simple analogy: "Rain is like a shower for the sky!" and mimes turning on a shower.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Showing a picture of rain) "Is 'rainy' like water falling from the sky?" (Yes/No response, or thumbs up/down).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Pointing to an umbrella) "What do we use for 'rainy' weather?" (Expect "umbrella" or pointing gesture).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Can we show 'sunny' with our hands?" (Students mime a sun).</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5-7 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "This Beautiful Day")</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> Short, illustrated weather messages (e.g., picture of a shining sun with "It's sunny. I'm happy!" and a picture of clouds with "It's cloudy. I'm a little bored.") displayed on the board. A simple dialogue or audio clip of two children describing weather and how it makes them feel, including simple analogies (e.g., "Wind is like a giant fan!").</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Pragmatic: Describing weather and feelings: "I'm happy." "It's raining." Asking and answering about feelings politely: "How do you feel?" "I feel happy." Sociolinguistic: Expressing emotions and needs: Using polite phrases to describe personal experiences (e.g., "I feel happy, thank you." "I need help with your umbrella, please.").</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher Actions:</b> The teacher introduces simple weather messages. "Look at this picture. It's sunny. I'm happy!" (pointing to words and picture). "How do you feel when it's sunny?" Students respond. The teacher then plays an audio clip where a child explains "Rain is like big tears from the sky." The teacher asks, "What is rain like?" encouraging students to identify the simple analogy. The teacher emphasizes contractions like "It's" and "I'm" and models pronunciation of /s/ in "sunny" and /r/ in "raining", and blending /r-eɪn/ for "rain."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist questions:</b> "What is the boy talking about in the audio? Weather or food?" (Weather). "How does he feel about the rain?" (Sad/Happy).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Information matching:</b> Match weather pictures (sun, rain, cloud) to feeling words (happy, sad, bored). Students point to the correct feeling.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">8-10 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Accuracy focus on Mediation)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Students will practice interpreting simple weather messages and explaining basic weather concepts using gestures and simple analogies. Grammar: Contractions ("I'm", "it's"), WH-questions ("How do you feel today?"), Present simple ("I feel happy on sunny days."). Vocabulary: sun, rain, cloud, wind, weather, umbrella, sky, coat, boots, shine, fall, blow, get wet, dry, enjoy, feel, play, sunny, rainy, cloudy, bright, warm, cold, windy, happy, sad, bored, excited, lazy, hot.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Controlled Practice: Weather Message Matching:</b> The teacher gives students a worksheet with two columns. Column A has simple sentences (e.g., "It's sunny. I'm happy." "It's rainy. I need my umbrella.") and Column B has matching weather icons and facial expressions. Students draw lines to match the sentence to the correct visual. This mediates the text into a visual representation.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Semi-controlled Practice: Analogy Charades:</b> The teacher says a weather word (e.g., "windy," "rainy," "sunny"). Students work in small groups to think of a simple analogy (e.g., "Wind is like a noisy sigh!") and then mime it. Other groups guess the weather word and the analogy if possible. The teacher encourages "Rain is like a shower," "Sun is like a big light bulb." This practices mediating concepts into actions and simple explanations.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Pair Interaction: "Explain the Weather Card":</b> Students work in pairs. Each pair receives a set of cards with weather words (e.g., "sunny", "cloudy", "rainy"). Student A picks a card, shows it to Student B, and Student B has to explain what the weather is like *without using the word on the card*. They use gestures, mimes, simple analogies, or descriptions (e.g., for "sunny": "It's warm. The big yellow light is in the sky. I feel happy!"). Student A then confirms. They switch roles.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">15-18 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "This Beautiful Day")</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> "Weather & Feelings Journal" Presentation and Interpretation. Students will present their own journal entries and also mediate a peer's entry to the class.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Each student takes out their "Weather & Feelings Journal" which contains drawings of daily weather and simple sentences (e.g., "It's sunny. I'm happy.").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">In small groups (3-4 students), each student takes a turn presenting one entry from their journal. They show their drawing and read their sentence. Then, they *mediate* their message further by using gestures (e.g., pointing to the sun for "It's sunny," miming happiness for "I'm happy") and simple analogies if applicable (e.g., "Wind is like a big blow!").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">After each presentation, another student in the group is chosen to *interpret* their peer's journal entry back to the group or the teacher, saying, "Today, [Friend's Name]'s journal says it's [weather] and [he/she] feels [feeling]." They might also be asked, "What did [Friend's Name] say 'rain' was like?" and they have to recall the analogy or gesture. This involves interpreting information from a peer's text/visual and re-explaining it.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher circulates, observing and providing support, encouraging polite questions ("How do you feel today, thank you?") and correct usage of contractions and vocabulary.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">10-12 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Formative assessment strategy)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students present one entry from their "Weather & Feelings Journal" to the teacher or a peer, orally explaining the weather and their feeling using words and gestures/mimes. They will also interpret a simple weather message (e.g., a teacher's drawing or a flashcard) by explaining it to the teacher using an analogy or gestures.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria (Observation Checklist):</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Intelligible pronunciation:</b> /s/ in "sunny," /r/ in "raining" and blending of phonemes.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Use of target vocabulary:</b> Uses at least 3-4 weather and feeling words correctly (e.g., sunny, happy, rain, sad).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Clarity of explanation (Mediation):</b> Clearly explains the weather/feeling in their journal entry using gestures AND words.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Analogy/Gesture use (Mediation):</b> Explains a weather concept with a simple analogy (e.g., "Rain is like a shower.") or appropriate gestures (e.g., points to sun for "It's sunny.").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Understanding interpretation (Mediation):</b> Can interpret a simple weather message (e.g., a flashcard) by explaining it to the teacher.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete a quick exit ticket with:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Today, I can explain 'sunny' by ____ (draw a picture or write 1-2 words like 'pointing to the sun')."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"I can tell my friend how I feel on a rainy day. I feel ____ (happy/sad/bored)."</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">8-10 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was easy to explain about the weather today?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What is one new way you learned to tell someone about the weather or how you feel?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What aspects of explaining weather and feelings (mediation) did students grasp well? Were the analogies effective?</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What specific weather words or feelings were challenging for students to mediate or interpret? What adjustments (e.g., more visual aids, simpler analogies, more TPR) are needed for future mediation tasks?</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson in the theme sequence:</b> (This is the final lesson in the sequence for this theme.) This lesson concludes the "Weather & Feelings Journal" project. The next theme will introduce new vocabulary and scenarios, building on the communicative skills developed here, perhaps requiring mediation between simple personal preferences.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">4-5 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>